// `JsConversionReason`'s home, for the reason `JsPathSegment.hex` is
// `JsPathSegment`'s: `spec/ffi.md` section 12's extension makes all three
// constructors qualified-only — `JsConversionReason.Shape`, never a bare
// `Shape` — and a qualified constructor is spelled through the module that
// declares it (Modules section 3.3).
//
// `Shape` and `Range` are the two words this buys back. Both are ordinary names
// for a user's own declaration, and `Range` is already a prelude *type*; a bare
// prelude constructor under either spelling is occluded only by a user
// declaration in the *constructor* namespace, so a user's `union Shape = Circle
// | Square` would leave the prelude's `Shape` standing in term position and any
// resulting message would name a union that appears nowhere in the program.

(** Why a checked conversion failed.

    `Shape` is "not the required kind of thing" — a wrong `typeof`, not an
    array, not an object where one was required. `Range` is the right kind of
    value outside the representable domain, which is why `toInt` on `2^53` is
    not the same answer as `toInt` on `"12"`. `Cycle` is a traversal that had to
    terminate meeting a back-edge; its payload is where the structure was first
    seen, which together with the failure's own path names the back-edge.

    Every constructor is reached qualified: `JsConversionReason.Shape`,
    `JsConversionReason.Range`, `JsConversionReason.Cycle(firstSeen)`, in
    expressions and in patterns alike. *)
export union JsConversionReason =
    | Shape
    | Range
    | Cycle(firstSeen: Vector(JsPathSegment))
